WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.950
Film Courage: When you began writing did you
feel like it came naturally to you?

00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:08.490
Was it something you felt you needed to look
at certain screenwriting books or more?

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:10.410
Nadia Jordan, Director, Producer, Actor: No.

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:13.060
I actually did buy a screenwriting book but
I never read it.

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:15.300
It was one of those things.

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:22.170
My writing partner had been on several courses
and had studied screenwriting so she knew

00:00:22.170 --> 00:00:24.260
the format more than I did.

00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:31.990
But for me I think I just had such a strong
idea of what I wanted and the story and I

00:00:31.990 --> 00:00:37.320
knew things about arcs and how I needed to
hit this and was very aware of when I was

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:40.790
writing it how it would film and budgets.

00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:50.360
At one point it I put it through script doctors
and get feedback on it.

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:54.890
It was really interesting if there were some
things that I was nervous about, if it came

00:00:54.890 --> 00:00:56.760
back, then I would change it.

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:02.170
Everyone has an opinion and whoever you give
a script to…it’s easy to pick scripts

00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:04.019
apart.

00:01:04.019 --> 00:01:08.970
I would give the script to people that I knew
would be ultra-critical because I don’t

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:11.040
want someone to tell me “Oh it’s a great
script!”

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:15.360
For the sake of it, give it to your hardest
critics and if they pick up on something that

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:18.720
you’re not sure about, then you know to
change it.

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:20.190
That’s what I did.

00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:21.500
I gave it to a lot of people.

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:25.400
I know a lot of established writers and I
would give it to them.

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:28.310
My writing partner would have a meltdown every
time it came back.

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:32.460
She would take it more personally but I was
just looking at it from a business side, making

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:33.460
a good film.

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:36.220
I don’t care what you say about my writing.

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:39.960
If something is not working…and it actually
changed a lot with the characters.

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:43.950
If some character was coming across a little
less likable than another, we would go in

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:45.170
and change that.

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:49.670
Or if some characters needed a bit more backstory
and they were a bit more one-dimensional we

00:01:49.670 --> 00:01:52.970
would change that.

00:01:52.970 --> 00:02:00.030
I think I just had a very strong idea of where
I wanted it to go and was just open to having

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:04.330
input and really wanted to make the best script
possible so whatever that took.

00:02:04.330 --> 00:02:06.800
Film Courage: Have you always been open to
input?

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:11.470
Because that’s a great idea in terms of
so many people they feel crushed with their

00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:14.690
first project and then they never want to
do it again.

00:02:14.690 --> 00:02:16.590
Nadia: No.

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:17.590
The thing with me…it’s funny.

00:02:17.590 --> 00:02:20.650
I can’t decide what I want to have for lunch
today.

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:25.910
But when it comes to work, some things I just
know that this is how I want to do it and

00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:27.520
nothing will waiver against that.

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:30.210
It was the same when I was in the edit process.

00:02:30.210 --> 00:02:34.390
Sometimes you just know things are working
and you know when they are not and if it’s

00:02:34.390 --> 00:02:39.670
something I’m insure about or I don’t
know about then yes, of course I want input

00:02:39.670 --> 00:02:40.670
from everyone.

00:02:40.670 --> 00:02:42.450
I think that’s basically how I got through
this film.

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:48.380
I had a couple of great people looking at
the script for me and a couple of producers

00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:54.030
helping me out with things I might not have
known on the producing side.

00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:56.730
I’m always open.

00:02:56.730 --> 00:03:02.140
Sometimes I might go with my own idea if I
have a very strong feeling about something.

00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:06.830
But I think that’s the key to making a good
film is collaboration right.

00:03:06.830 --> 00:03:08.980
You don’t want to do it all your way.

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:11.910
If there is good people around you with good
input then listen.

00:03:11.910 --> 00:03:17.120
Film Courage: At what point did you take it
to a script doctor or script doctors (I don’t

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:18.760
know how many people you actually had see
it)?

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:21.250
Nadia: I don’t think it was actually a script
doctor.

00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:22.730
I think it’s script coverage.

00:03:22.730 --> 00:03:25.940
So you put it through this service and they
read it.

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:29.760
They don’t change anything or write anything
for you but they will read it and give you

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:30.760
feedback.

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:31.990
A lot of the studios will use it.

00:03:31.990 --> 00:03:36.840
They will put their scripts through and have
people read it and give them marks and marks

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:41.130
off for this and is it funny and how would
you describe it.

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:45.290
I think I put it through two or three and
it wasn’t until I was really happy with

00:03:45.290 --> 00:03:46.830
it…not really happy with it.

00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:52.400
We’d gone as far as we could at that point
and then it was okay let’s just see how

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:54.230
it reads to somebody else.

00:03:54.230 --> 00:04:00.450
Film Courage: Were there points where you
were surprised that with the coverage notes

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:03.280
they really loved something or they didn’t
love something?

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:07.390
Nadia: There were no real surprises to me
actually.

00:04:07.390 --> 00:04:10.720
There were things that I knew we still needed
to work on.

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:16.351
It’s always interesting though with a script,
with an edit, with the film (anything) what

00:04:16.351 --> 00:04:19.220
appeals to different people.

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:24.210
You watch a film with one audience and the
whole room seems to laugh at different things

00:04:24.210 --> 00:04:26.790
than what an audience will laugh at a another
night.

00:04:26.790 --> 00:04:28.220
And they don’t know.

00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:31.620
How are they laughing at completely different
things.

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:34.030
And it was the same with the script.

00:04:34.030 --> 00:04:37.419
People would pick up these things and it was
like Well that’s not really funny.

00:04:37.419 --> 00:04:41.240
That wasn’t meant to be funny but great
if you think it’s funny then that’s probably

00:04:41.240 --> 00:04:43.000
a good thing.

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:47.620
And there would be other things that people
would pick up that I just felt that okay that’s

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:54.540
just their opinion so it really depended on
the kind of comments we got back to how personally

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:58.930
or whether I was going to take them and actually
make a change from that.

